emy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referenc