re-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8b3cb19393ca2d1dae18471d1d2d6670899615a815a11682196d00030265b43" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/state.json" "file_sha256" "43e2663861d33981edf6ff452aaf3cd99ffad028ec55be9955a66cff6946df68" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "353ef04985c2ed3b0a3d725f72745305fe74becfdffccd10c47917df27672261" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "353ef04985c2ed3b0a3d725f72745305fe74becfdffccd10c47917df27672261" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\n- For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-8229.json" "file_sha256" "590a37215ed160d64c92139a129f2105e2f41750bd789ecfd65151b4416cc770" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/example-8229.json" "file_sha256" "590a37215ed160d64c92139a129f2105e2f41750bd789ecfd65151b4416cc770" "basis" 1647 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ec8b0187e16321f740438c53111801032538dd58e77b42951feed86a4c52cd4" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c