r any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ec8b0187e16321f740438c53111801032538dd58e77b42951feed86a4c52cd4" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a70b989c97c45b573ed146e4d45ea0125ef31cbf2f6f0ed508238fe51f0edb5" "basis" 2658 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072828/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-43859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-160861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in N,$ where $p(x)={x}^{3}-1.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n- The set is closed under addition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162379" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $G$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero matrix $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $G.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in G,$ we have $A-O=A.$\n- For any $A,B\\in G,$ we have $A-B=B-A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162620" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))=2p(x)-2q(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-162665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-106806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e7550422f0227dfcffde9806fd8330888f51dea4d9de4365e8ea1e79261192c"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "q-94723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4c51d18876b3d9e168e0e12d1a68a7ea2a19520298d2b332af62124f288cc8d"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "c7b9c90d2f7ce2328e8aa6a6461f5fa15e6f045348ea527dbc05637fa17f8558"} {"question" "e-9960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-9960.json" "file_sha256" "c7b9c90d2f7ce2328e8aa6a6461f5fa15e6f045348ea527dbc05637fa17f8558"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa"} {"question" "e-4787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-4787.json" "file_sha256" "6de4f28b3cb76e6eccc9ab114eb9a0eefdc8b9873f06f27449a851d1a6b69efa"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\n- For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-8229.json" "file_sha256" "590a37215ed160d64c92139a129f2105e2f41750bd789ecfd65151b4416cc770"} {"question" "e-8229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/example-8229.json" "file_sha256" "590a37215ed160d64c92139a129f2105e2f41750bd789ecfd65151b4416cc770"}] "usage" {"e-9960" [] "q-94740" [] "q-160866" [] "q-94723" [] "q-106806" [] "q-160861" [] "q-106688" [] "q-94694" [] "e-8229" [] "q-94649" [] "q-43859" [] "e-4787" []} "basis" 2881 "retractions" [[17592186125158 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0, ∞)$ to $R$ of the form $f(x) = a\\log_{2} (x)$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n2. For any $α, β ∈ R$ and $f(x) ∈ L$, we have $(α + β)f(x) = αf(x) + βf(x)$.\n3. For any $α ∈ R$ and any $f(x), g(x) ∈ L$, we have $α(f(x) + g(x)) = αf(x) + αg(x)$."] [17592186124350 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121130 17592186121131 17592186121132 17592186121133 17592186121134 17